104 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 69 0 R
/E (The Hospital Patient Record System is a console-based Java application developed under Problem Statement No. 36 at Rungta International Skills University to maintain structured patient records in a single-file program \(HospitalApp.java\). In small clinics or front-desk environments, keeping track of patient details and locating records quickly requires a lightweight tool that avoids unnecessary setup while preventing common data entry mistakes. The application models each patient as a Patient object encapsulating patientId, name, age, and disease, and stores active records dynamically in an ArrayList<Patient> during execution. Through an interactive menu loop, users can register new patients with automatic PT101-style sequential ID generation, display all registered patients, perform case-insensitive name searches, and sort records in ascending order of age using a custom bubble sort routine without built-in sorting helpers. Input checks enforce non-empty names and diseases along with valid ages between 0 and 120. Data persistence is handled via patients.txt using a pipe-delimited format \(patientId|name|age|disease\), loading existing records at startup \(while restoring the next ID counter\) and writing updates back to disk after modifications and on exit. Across normal, boundary \(age = 0\), invalid \(age = 200\), and multi-step scenarios, the system demonstrates core Java fundamentals including object-oriented encapsulation, dynamic collections, custom algorithms, exception handling, and file I/O. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
